  "steps": [
    "11.h.W: prepare document.create, section.add and section.move as closed ordinary typed operation sequences; save and execute through the shared native writer. Verify exact affected artifacts, unchanged file inventories, native revisions and audit, receipts and idempotent replay, actor/host denial, primary-role validity, specialist closure and isolated Core/PYZ publication.",
    "11.h.R: reject invalid parents, self/descendant/include cycles, stale relation hashes and actual on-disk bytes, identity reuse, forged operation shapes, missing lifecycle and host gate overrides. Preserve command/plan identity in structured failure audits. Verify unchanged full inventories on refusal and honest partial publication/recovery after an injected second-owner replace failure; conflicting retry preserves original receipt."
  ],
  "oracle": {
    "kind": "behavior",
    "requirement_contract": "Die Befehle `document create`, `section add`, `section move`, `section remove` und `block add`, `block update`, `block remove` MÜSSEN vollständiges nichtinteraktives Dokument-Authoring über den gemeinsamen Change Plan ermöglichen.",
    "scope": "Native document/section saved-plan authoring over complete supplied project inputs. Creation is an empty draft, addition creates and attaches a section atomically, and movement removes the hash-bound old contains edge and adds a fresh relation in one plan. Only affected relation owners change revision; original section/descendant and unrelated file bytes remain. Required native role/ownership/include checks cannot be replaced by a host gate. No presentation, documentation or fake semantic-service oracle. R retains the native invariant guards and closes the shared failure-adapter command registry for all three document commands. No complete-target approval or rollback is inferred from a failed write."
  },
